.TH SEND-UNSAFE 1 "September 2025" "MyWallet Toolkit 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
send-unsafe \- Bitcoin transaction sender with command-line private key (INSECURE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B send-unsafe.sh
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-n 
.IR network ]
.RB [ \-f 
.IR fee_rate ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-b 
.IR 0 | 1 ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.I from_address
.I to_address
.I amount_sat
[\fIPRIVATE_KEY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B send-unsafe.sh
creates and broadcasts Bitcoin transactions using private keys provided directly via command-line arguments or stdin. This is the 
.B INSECURE
version of the transaction sender that exposes private keys in process lists and command history.

.B CRITICAL SECURITY WARNING:
This tool accepts private keys as command-line arguments, making them visible in process lists, command history, and potentially logs. This is 
.B EXTREMELY INSECURE
and should only be used for educational purposes with test funds. For any real use, prefer send.sh with keyring authentication.

.B WARNING:
This tool is designed for educational and testing purposes only. Never use this script for production Bitcoin wallets or real funds. For actual Bitcoin storage and transactions, always use hardware wallets or certified software solutions.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet mode. Suppress warnings and progress information.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode. Show detailed transaction creation and broadcast process.
.TP
.B \-n \fInetwork\fR
Specify Bitcoin network. Supported values:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
.B mainnet
- Bitcoin main network
.IP \(bu 4
.B testnet
- Bitcoin test network
.RE
If not specified, auto-detects from network.sh configuration.
.TP
.B \-f \fIfee_rate\fR
Fee rate in satoshis per byte. Default is 10 sat/byte.
.TP
.B \-d
Dry run mode. Create and display transaction but do not broadcast to network.
.TP
.B \-b \fI0|1\fR
Broadcast transaction to network. 1=yes (default), 0=no. Automatically disabled in dry-run mode.
.TP
.B \-h
Show help message and exit.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.B from_address
Source Bitcoin address. Must match the address derived from the provided private key.
.TP
.B to_address
Destination Bitcoin address in any supported format (P2PKH, P2SH, P2WPKH, P2WSH).
.TP
.B amount_sat
Amount to send in satoshis (1 BTC = 100,000,000 satoshis).
.TP
.B private_key
Hex-encoded private key (64 characters). Can be provided as argument or via stdin.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B INSECURE: Command-line private key (visible in process list):
.nf
./send-unsafe.sh tb1qfrom... tb1qto... 50000 a1b2c3d4e5f6789...
.fi

.TP
Slightly less insecure: Private key via stdin:
.nf
echo 'a1b2c3d4e5f6789...' | ./send-unsafe.sh tb1qfrom... tb1qto... 50000
.fi

.TP
Dry run with custom fee rate:
.nf
./send-unsafe.sh -d -f 20 tb1qfrom... tb1qto... 100000 a1b2c3...
.fi

.TP
Verbose testnet transaction:
.nf
./send-unsafe.sh -v -n testnet tb1qfrom... tb1qto... 25000 a1b2c3...
.fi

.SH SECURITY RISKS
.B THIS TOOL IS INHERENTLY INSECURE. The following security risks exist:

.SS Process Visibility
.IP \(bu 4
Private keys visible in 'ps aux' and similar process lists
.IP \(bu 4
Keys stored in shell command history
.IP \(bu 4
Process arguments may be logged by system monitoring tools

.SS Memory Exposure
.IP \(bu 4
Private keys passed as arguments remain in process memory
.IP \(bu 4
No secure memory clearing
.IP \(bu 4
Potential for memory dumps to expose keys

.SS Log Contamination
.IP \(bu 4
Commands may be logged by shells, system logs, or monitoring tools
.IP \(bu 4
Keys may appear in crash dumps or core files
.IP \(bu 4
Debug information may inadvertently expose keys

.SS Multi-User Systems
.IP \(bu 4
Other users on the system can see command arguments
.IP \(bu 4
No protection against local privilege escalation
.IP \(bu 4
Shared system logs may expose private keys

.SH SAFER ALTERNATIVES
.TP
.B Recommended: Use send.sh with keyring
.nf
keyauth.sh send.sh from_addr to_addr amount
.fi

.TP
.B Better: Use send-secure.sh
.nf
keyauth-secure.sh send-secure.sh from_addr to_addr amount
.fi

.TP
.B Best: Use hardware wallets or certified software
For any real Bitcoin transactions.

.SH WHEN TO USE
This insecure tool should 
.B ONLY
be used for:
.IP \(bu 4
Educational demonstrations with worthless test keys
.IP \(bu 4
Automated testing with disposable keys
.IP \(bu 4
Academic research with test networks only
.IP \(bu 4
Understanding Bitcoin transaction mechanics

.B NEVER
use for:
.IP \(bu 4
Real Bitcoin with actual value
.IP \(bu 4
Production environments
.IP \(bu 4
Shared or multi-user systems
.IP \(bu 4
Any situation where security matters

.SH TRANSACTION PROCESS
The transaction creation process is identical to send.sh:

.IP 1. 4
Validate input addresses and private key
.IP 2. 4
Fetch UTXOs for from_address
.IP 3. 4
Select UTXOs and calculate fees
.IP 4. 4
Create and sign transaction
.IP 5. 4
Broadcast to network (if enabled)

.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B network.sh
Required for network configuration
.TP
.B balance.sh
Required for UTXO fetching
.TP
.B openssl
Required for transaction signing
.TP
.B curl
Required for blockchain API communication
.TP
.B jq
Required for JSON processing

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Transaction created and broadcast successfully
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments or private key
.TP
.B 2
Address validation failed
.TP
.B 3
Insufficient funds or UTXO errors
.TP
.B 4
Transaction creation or signing failed
.TP
.B 5
Broadcasting failed (network error)

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BITCOIN_NETWORK
Network override (mainnet/testnet)

.SH RESPONSIBLE DISCLOSURE
If you find this tool being used inappropriately:
.IP \(bu 4
Educate users about the security risks
.IP \(bu 4
Recommend secure alternatives
.IP \(bu 4
Report security incidents to appropriate authorities
.IP \(bu 4
Never exploit discovered private keys

